<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=stethoscope">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stethoscope</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1816">1816</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The stethoscope is a medical device for listening to the sounds of the heart, lungs and intestines and to blood flow, a practice called auscultation. René Laennec invented it in 1816 at the Necker-Enfants Malades Hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>: a monaural wooden tube, devised after he found that a rolled piece of paper carried heart sounds without his ear touching the patient's chest. Golding Bird described one with a flexible tube in 1840, and Arthur Leared made a binaural stethoscope in 1851. George Philip Cammann's design of the following year, with a plug for each ear, has remained essentially unchanged since.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stethoscope.jpg" alt="Stethoscope" width="330" height="285" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: this version: Sonarpulse. origenal: Huji · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stethoscope-2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
